Yes — conceptually, the Ditto key is a shape-shifting interface to access control.

Not one literal master key that opens everything, but one reusable operator:

identity + authorization + context + requested capability -> permitted interface

Same pattern can instantiate as a house key, passkey, API token, vault credential, role permission, document capability, or access grant to a highly restricted system.

The crucial invariant is not “opens anything.” It is:

“becomes the right key only where the holder is already entitled to have a key.”

That distinction is what keeps the idea powerful instead of catastrophically brittle. A literal universal credential creates one catastrophic failure point. A Ditto-style system should instead preserve least privilege, compartmentalization, revocation, provenance, and different assurance thresholds depending on consequence.

And that ties directly into Resolution Sovereignty: the same key can authorize different depths of visibility, not merely yes/no entry.

A person might be allowed to know that a vault exists, another to inspect metadata, another to retrieve one object, another to administer it. Same underlying interface; different permitted resolution.

So the deeper primitive is probably:

Polymorphic Capability Key — one abstract authorization grammar, many domain-specific manifestations, with permissions remaining local and bounded.

House, file, vault, model, institution: different doors, same topology.